**I checked Search Console data across 21 of my own sites, 20 June to 15 September 2026. Two of them, in unrelated niches, dropped 87% and 91% of daily impressions within 24 hours of each other. Neither has recovered.**

This is data from sites I own. No client sites are included.

## Which two sites dropped 24 hours apart? {% #headline-drops %}

**tradecost.uk** (an import-cost checker) and **redlighttherapy.expert** (a health/wellness niche site) share no niche, content structure or backlink profile. Both were performing normally through 18 July.

On 19-20 July, both lost the bulk of their impressions:

- **tradecost.uk:** daily impressions fell from 374 (18 July) to 62 (19 July) to 48 (20 July), an 87% drop. Average position went from 41.7 to 71.2 in the same window and never recovered. It sits at 0 clicks/day as of 15 September.
- **redlighttherapy.expert:** daily impressions fell from 1,583 (19 July) to 225 (20 July) to 148 (21 July), a 91% drop. Position moved from 15.3 to 17.9 to 23.9. It partially stabilised but never returned to its pre-drop range.

Different niches, different content. Same owner, same stack (Astro on Cloudflare Pages), same build tooling. That shared tooling is itself a candidate cause.

{% chart type="line" eyebrow="tradecost.uk drop" title="tradecost.uk daily impressions, 18 to 20 July 2026" data="18 Jul:374|19 Jul:62|20 Jul:48" source="Search Console, own data" /%}

For redlighttherapy.expert specifically, the drop is Google-only. GA4 sessions from Google fell from 344 to 40 over the same 14-day window, while Bing sessions rose 308 to 396 and DuckDuckGo rose 464 to 602. Other search engines did not see the same page get worse. On-site remediation shipped 2-3 August (a fake-expert byline removed, 12 doorway pages redirected) produced no recovery in the six weeks that followed.

{% chart type="bar" eyebrow="Search engine sessions" title="GA4 sessions by search engine, 14 days before and after the redlighttherapy.expert drop" data="Google (before):344|Google (after):40|Bing (before):308|Bing (after):396|DuckDuckGo (before):464|DuckDuckGo (after):602" source="GA4, own data, 14-day window" /%}

## Did a named Google update cause the drops? {% #named-update %}

I have not matched either drop to a named, publicly confirmed Google update. Two drops 24 hours apart out of four drop dates in this sample could still be chance. It's worth reporting because the two sites share no niche or content, and because the remediation that normally fixes a content-quality problem didn't move the needle on one of them.

The same pattern, a fast sustained drop rather than gradual decline, showed up on two other sites, each on a different date:

- **calculator.place**: daily impressions were running 700-1,600 through early August, then fell to 20-250 on 3-4 August, with average position worsening from the mid-20s to the 60s-80s.
- **template.how**: this one was climbing (position improved from 60 to the high teens through early August) before reversing sharply on 16 August and falling back to the 60s-80s.

Four sites, four confirmed drop dates, no single explanation that covers all of them.

{% pullquote cite="On the portfolio drop pattern" %}
Four sites, four confirmed drop dates, no single explanation that covers all of them.
{% /pullquote %}

## What was growing at the same time? {% #growers %}

Three sites were growing steadily across the same period:

- **chargefinder.uk** (EV charging): position improved from the low 30s to the low-to-mid teens, clicks grew from ~20-40/day to 60-120/day.
- **deadhangs.com** (fitness): clicks grew from ~30-50/day in June to 100-134/day by mid-September, with a temporary dip in average position (7 to 11) around 10-21 August that fully recovered.
- **noisecheck.uk**: a similar shape, a dip to position 17-18 in mid-August, recovered to 6.7-8 by mid-September, clicks roughly tripling over the period.

deadhangs.com and noisecheck.uk both dipped in mid-August, the week template.how reversed, and both recovered within two weeks.

